# Normalise line endings in the working tree, independent of platform.
#
# ── Why this file is not optional ──
#
# Without a policy, `core.autocrlf=true` on Windows writes CRLF into checked-out
# files while the repository stores LF. Anything that compares file contents
# byte-wise then sees different bytes on different machines.
#
# That is not hypothetical here: a guard in this project determined whether a
# destructive call sat inside a conditional by slicing source text at offsets
# computed from `\n`. On a CRLF checkout every offset was one byte short, the
# condition was read as empty, and the test failed deterministically on Windows
# while passing everywhere else. The fix made the guard line-ending agnostic —
# but the deeper fix is to stop line endings from varying at all.
#
# `text=auto` lets git decide per file, `eol=lf` normalises everything git treats
# as text, and the explicit binary list prevents git from "helpfully" rewriting
# audio, image or archive files.
* text=auto eol=lf

# ── Source and docs: always LF ──
*.ts    text eol=lf
*.tsx   text eol=lf
*.js    text eol=lf
*.mjs   text eol=lf
*.cjs   text eol=lf
*.json  text eol=lf
*.md    text eol=lf
*.css   text eol=lf
*.html  text eol=lf
*.yml   text eol=lf
*.yaml  text eol=lf
*.svg   text eol=lf

# ── Shell scripts must keep LF even on Windows, or they will not execute ──
*.sh    text eol=lf
*.bash  text eol=lf

# ── Windows-native scripts need CRLF to run under cmd.exe / PowerShell ──
*.ps1   text eol=crlf
*.cmd   text eol=crlf
*.bat   text eol=crlf

# ── Binary: never inspect, never convert ──
*.png   binary
*.jpg   binary
*.jpeg  binary
*.gif   binary
*.webp  binary
*.ico   binary
*.pdf   binary
*.docx  binary
*.mp3   binary
*.wav   binary
*.m4a   binary
*.zip   binary
*.gz    binary
*.woff  binary
*.woff2 binary
*.ttf   binary
*.otf   binary
*.db    binary
*.sqlite binary

# ── Lockfiles: mark as generated so diffs and reviews treat them accordingly ──
pnpm-lock.yaml linguist-generated=true

# ── Keep the release tooling visible in diffs ──
#
# The release tooling is ordinary source: it is reviewed like any other code,
# and a change to it must be as visible in a diff as a change to `src/`.
# (The export *policy* is deliberately not part of this repository — it names
# paths from the internal tree. See README § Repository policy.)
tools/*.mjs -linguist-documentation
